{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1495191-musket-mechanics","id":1495191,"title":"Musket mechanics","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-10-18T14:33:57-07:00","id":1495191,"title":"Musket mechanics","content":"I have some players interested in playing a campaign during the Napoleonic Wars. Does anyone have mechanics for smoothbore pistols or muskets? Or know of any existing? Historically, a well trained soldier can fire, load, and fire again in about 20 seconds. An experienced soldier can do it in about 15. Pistols, with shorter barrels, can be faster. My instinct is to add a full turn or action with celerity, to load, or even 2 actions, depending on Firearms scores. Thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27640-lasombramagister","name":"LasombraMagister","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T15:37:48-07:00","id":1495197,"title":"","content":"Ultimately, you have to make a decision about how to balance making them feel antique, and keeping it fun. The default combat round in the WoD games assumes 3 to 5 seconds. That would mean 15 seconds is three rounds (not just three actions). That is, of course, not great for the flow of combat to have characters sit there and reload for 3/4s of their turns.<br /> <br /> Of course, Napoleonic Warfare is built around mass infantry formations so you can cycle firing lines. When you get to small scale personal combat firearms were generally fired ones in a fight and either won, or the fight moved to other weapons (or sometimes people would carry multiple loaded firearms).<br /> <br /> So... what do you really want to have in play? Are the PCs going to be part of large scale battles where reducing the firearms reloading on a musket too much might break immersion? Or is that a backdrop and you could really make guns a single use weapon because small scale personal combat is going to preclude reloading as a realistic option (Celerity is an iffy thing because you need to decide how much you want to care about physicals and tamping down black powder with Celerity level speeds).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T16:30:29-07:00","id":1495202,"title":"","content":"It's a bit dated, but there are basic mechanics for era-specific weapons in WoD: Combat. Musket balls were pretty big compared to, say, bullets from a rifle, so wounds would be closer to a shotgun slug than smaller bullets, at least if the musket could reach the target with its rather low-quality ballistics and tendency to curve away from where you aim it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T05:19:06-07:00","id":1495236,"title":"","content":"Muzzle loading firearms have always been tricky for role playing games. The damage shouldn't be significantly different from modern firearms, though the range would be reduced, especially if it is smooth bore. The bullets are bigger but they are move more slowly. The tricky part is reloading. Reloading such a gun is essentially impossible in close combat. If someone gets close to you and you have an unloaded muzzle loader, then it's time to fix bayonets, draw your side sword, or use it as a club.<br /> <br /> In a combat situation with close combat, I would say that it's essentially impossible to load the gun. Maybe with Celerity, but in that case, you'd probably be better off making multiple close combat attacks rather than loading your gun. With ranged combat, it might work to treat loading the gun as an extended action with a large number of successes required. Maybe roll Wits + Firearms and you need 20 successes. If you have a high Wits and Firearms, then maybe you can load the gun in several rounds, otherwise it could take a really long time. Celerity or Time magick could make it possible to do this much faster.<br /> <br /> In most RPGs that include muzzle loaders, they make it way easier to reload the gun than it would be in real life, essentially because it's more fun to be able to use the guns in combat more than once.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T08:24:29-07:00","id":1495244,"title":"","content":"3 or 4 rounds doesn't sound too off. If both sides are reloading, you might have fun skipping those rounds and just rolling wits or courage+firearms to see who can shoot first. <br /> <br /> Celerity really is too questionable if you want to be more on the simulationist side of things; you can do things multiplicatively faster but it's just as easy to defend against a man swinging five times as it is to defend against a man who swings once (before we give you penalities for multiple defences that is)<br /> <br /> Just like a human, A vampire with celerity would have better luck bringing multiple firearms with him. Vampires have some small reprieve in that they don't need to be so cautious with the gun as they can handily survive misfires and misuse and so maybe some shortcuts can be taken at times<br /> <br /> A vampire with celerity and potence could work wonders with a crossbow. If you can use a hand where you'd need a lever, or a lever where you'd need a crank, you'd output far more hurt than you ever could with a gun. One of the ironies of the era is that people spent hundreds of years picking slower firing weapons to beat armour, and now that very few bother with armour they're all still using slower weapons. (and there's lots of complexities behind that but broad stroaks it's kinda interesting.) <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A general advice that stays true: guns are loud and shooting should be avoided. vampires are urban creatures after all. Have Suprise and either end it in a turn or two or be blood buffed to 6/6/6 or beyond so that nothing can stop your rampage. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T11:06:50-07:00","id":1495254,"title":"","content":"Smooth bore muskets also have quite the accuracy issue, which is about half the reason they were used in volley fire, the other half being reloading speed. Essentially the musket ball bounces down the barrel of a smooth bore which means when it exits the muzzle it can take any trajectory in a wide cone, and the bounce pattern changes with every shot. This is critical to understand when trying to create mechanics for these types of weapons. Modern smooth barrel shotguns don't equate in firing pattern unless you are using bird shot or buck shot, and even then, with a musket you only have one projectile.<br /> <br /> As for loading and firing....<br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lfGOLqxcbIg\" target=\"_blank\">Loading and Firing a Flintlock Musket</a><br /> <br /> As you can see, this is a very time-consuming process. Hence why swords and bayonets were so crucial at the time. You fire and then hope the enemy doesn't close distance while you are reloading. Bear in mind that the video is showing a more steamlined version of musketry than what was used during the Napoleonic wars.<br /> <br /> Mechanically the reload would charitably be about 4 rounds, with rolls to succeed at each step of the way. As the video mentions, you break or lose your ramrod, the weapon is useless. Weather conditions affect if the powder is viable, which means since you are in the WoD setting, it is some form of raining or heavy fog at all times.<br /> <br /> So rather than trying to build mechanics, it would probably be better to build tactics. The characters will have a melee weapon, a long gun, and a few pistols. They open fire, hopefully from defensive position and from ambush. Once the first volley is fired, you drop your musket and charge the enemy. You use your pistols to keep their heads down until you can get into melee range.<br /> <br /> As far as damage goes, there wasn't much standardization back then, but on average the musket balls were about 68 caliber in size. Yes, bigger than the 50 cal Desert Eagle. So while it didn't have modern propellants, it was a reasonable amount of mass hitting the target. Very little chance of over penetration, but more than enough energy to bounce around a body cavity.<br /> <br /> Of course all of this is before you have to factor in the skill and experience of the shooter. While this is true of most things, it takes a certain kind of person to do complex actions like reloading while standing in formation while being shot at.<br /> <br /> While vampires mostly won't care about being shot, there is a bigger issue with black powder weapons. If vampires react to things like cigarette lighters, they are going to react to the flash pan right next to their face and the resulting explosion at the end of the barrel. I can see your average vampire using pistols rather than muskets just to keep frenzy out of the picture.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":727,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","user":"Thoth","date":". 10-19-2022, 02:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-21T19:48:57-07:00","id":1495401,"title":"","content":"I just used the archaic-rifle out of WtO and made my characters roll for each action they had to do to fire the weapon again, such as cleaning, reload the bullet and loading the powder all with modifiers due to combat/stress. It was actually a little amusing to hear my players complain that they had to do all these steps to fire a gun as they were used to modern-guns.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}